1
00:00:06,631 --> 00:00:17,159
[SPEAKER_04]: So, regarding the training of the mind, we might have discussed in the past how it's not something that you can do directly.

2
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:27,346
[SPEAKER_04]: And we had that discussion about seeing the signs of the mind, which is not the same as seeing the mind.

3
00:00:27,786 --> 00:00:32,950
[SPEAKER_04]: Colloquially, you might say that way, but there's a reason why the Buddha said you see the signs of the mind.

4
00:00:32,990 --> 00:00:33,791
[SPEAKER_04]: He didn't say you

5
00:00:35,495 --> 00:00:39,698
[SPEAKER_04]: Kind of grasp the mind as you would grasp an object with your eyes or your ears.

6
00:00:40,838 --> 00:00:47,543
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in the same way, when it comes to training the mind, it's also an indirect principle.

7
00:00:48,403 --> 00:00:55,168
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, that's one of the reasons why, let's say, you have like a sensual thought or an averse thought.

8
00:00:56,183 --> 00:01:10,647
[SPEAKER_04]: The natural tendency would be, how do I just think the opposite, antidote it, get rid of it, suppress it, forget about it, ignore it, in some kind... but in that direct sense.

9
00:01:11,587 --> 00:01:18,229
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just the content of that perception or image that you have in your mind, you just want to get rid of it in whichever way you can.

10
00:01:19,170 --> 00:01:23,911
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that would be the direct way of trying to deal with things, which is

11
00:01:25,202 --> 00:01:33,656
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is wrong because it fails to to account for that indirect relationship between you and the mind.

12
00:01:35,427 --> 00:01:44,550
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is kind of like... you could compare it to an animal, as the Buddha did in the Sutras quite often, with an elephant.

13
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:55,493
[SPEAKER_04]: When the elephant is doing something, you know, it's... you just took it out of the jungle, and it's rebelling against what you're telling it to do, and it wants to go back.

14
00:01:56,453 --> 00:02:07,276
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't just, like, forcefully, out of some magical technique or something, get the elephant to do what you want right away.

15
00:02:08,077 --> 00:02:11,858
[SPEAKER_04]: Instead, it's by not allowing it to do what it wants.

16
00:02:11,938 --> 00:02:14,879
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as the Buddha would say, tying it down with the leather straps.

17
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:26,877
[SPEAKER_04]: And not letting it go where it wants, that you would indirectly wear away its wildness until it becomes responsive to your commands.

18
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:42,847
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there's basically... there's one sutta that I think isn't very well known, even though it very acutely describes this principle of taming the mind.

19
00:02:43,467 --> 00:02:45,748
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's Majjhima Nikaya No.

20
00:02:45,888 --> 00:02:52,512
[SPEAKER_04]: 8, and basically it starts with this... it's...

21
00:02:55,102 --> 00:03:08,762
[SPEAKER_04]: A monk asks a question, basically saying that, okay, if a monk just sticks to the basics of the training, like the virtue, the sense restraint, is it that, you know, his wrong views will be abandoned?

22
00:03:10,334 --> 00:03:12,856
[SPEAKER_04]: And the Buddha says, no, it's not just that.

23
00:03:13,016 --> 00:03:26,785
[SPEAKER_04]: It says, a bhikkhu gives up and lets go of these views by seeing as it is, with right understanding, where they arise, where they settle, and where they operate, as this is not mine, I am not this, this is not myself.

24
00:03:29,106 --> 00:03:30,207
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the basic principle.

25
00:03:30,267 --> 00:03:35,631
[SPEAKER_04]: And then he says, talks about the four jhānas and says,

26
00:03:36,428 --> 00:03:44,292
[SPEAKER_04]: A monk might be thinking that because, you know, he's attaining those states, that he's practicing wearing away.

27
00:03:45,453 --> 00:03:48,875
[SPEAKER_04]: But in the training of the Noble One, these are not called wearing away.

28
00:03:49,355 --> 00:03:53,097
[SPEAKER_04]: They're called simply pleasant abidings in the present experience.

29
00:03:54,397 --> 00:03:57,519
[SPEAKER_04]: So that applies to all of the samādhi attainments.

30
00:03:57,719 --> 00:04:04,383
[SPEAKER_04]: And then the Buddha goes on to say, so what is it that... or how is it that you practice wearing away?

31
00:04:05,510 --> 00:04:10,711
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it says, others will be cruel, but here we will not be cruel.

32
00:04:11,732 --> 00:04:15,753
[SPEAKER_04]: Others will kill living creatures, but here we will not kill living creatures.

33
00:04:16,533 --> 00:04:18,534
[SPEAKER_04]: Others will steal, but here we will not steal.

34
00:04:19,514 --> 00:04:22,455
[SPEAKER_04]: Others will not be celibate, but here we will be celibate.

35
00:04:22,475 --> 00:04:30,377
[SPEAKER_04]: Basically, it goes on to all sorts of unwholesome behaviors that other people might do, but you will not do.

36
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:32,480
[SPEAKER_04]: Others will be resentful.

37
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:34,041
[SPEAKER_04]: Here, we will not be resentful.

38
00:04:34,221 --> 00:04:35,541
[SPEAKER_04]: Here, others will denigrate.

39
00:04:36,042 --> 00:04:37,142
[SPEAKER_04]: Here, we will not denigrate.

40
00:04:37,742 --> 00:04:43,584
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, it ends with others will be attached to their own views, holding them tight and refusing to let go.

41
00:04:44,525 --> 00:04:50,887
[SPEAKER_04]: But here, we will not be attached to our own views, not holding them tight, but we will... but we'll let them go easily.

42
00:04:52,667 --> 00:04:55,909
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it gives these different expositions of the same principle.

43
00:04:56,749 --> 00:04:59,890
[SPEAKER_04]: But the last one really is the most interesting one.

44
00:05:01,411 --> 00:05:05,434
[SPEAKER_04]: It's called the exposition... exposition by quenching.

45
00:05:05,494 --> 00:05:07,116
[SPEAKER_04]: In Nepali is parinibbāna.

46
00:05:08,457 --> 00:05:17,263
[SPEAKER_04]: And it says, if you're sinking in the mud yourself, chunda, it is quite impossible for you to pull out someone else who is sinking in the mud.

47
00:05:18,104 --> 00:05:23,808
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you're not sinking in the mud yourself, it is quite possible for you to pull out someone else who is sinking in the mud.

48
00:05:24,589 --> 00:05:27,651
[SPEAKER_04]: If you are not tamed, trained and quenched yourself,

49
00:05:28,397 --> 00:05:32,038
[SPEAKER_04]: It is quite impossible for you to help tame, train, and quench someone else.

50
00:05:32,839 --> 00:05:39,061
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you are tamed, trained, and quench yourself, it is quite possible for you to help tame, train, and quench someone else.

51
00:05:40,742 --> 00:05:44,683
[SPEAKER_04]: In the same way, a cruel individual quenches it by not being cruel.

52
00:05:45,543 --> 00:05:48,425
[SPEAKER_04]: An individual who kills quenches it by not killing.

53
00:05:49,945 --> 00:05:51,886
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it just repeats these same things.

54
00:05:54,307 --> 00:05:54,847
[SPEAKER_04]: And, eh...

55
00:05:57,206 --> 00:06:07,672
[SPEAKER_04]: It ends with that common or, well, well-known instruction that the Buddha would give that says, here are the roots of trees, here are the empty huts.

56
00:06:08,652 --> 00:06:09,493
[SPEAKER_04]: Develop jhāna.

57
00:06:09,813 --> 00:06:10,593
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't be negligent.

58
00:06:10,753 --> 00:06:11,914
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't regret it later.

59
00:06:12,294 --> 00:06:13,215
[SPEAKER_04]: This is my instruction.

60
00:06:14,455 --> 00:06:24,201
[SPEAKER_04]: Which, he would give that kind of statement usually when he kind of explains something very fundamental to the practice.

61
00:06:24,221 --> 00:06:24,481
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

62
00:06:26,903 --> 00:06:39,641
[SPEAKER_04]: As it says there, it gives that... there's obviously like a mundane application of what he just said there, that's... okay, if you don't understand the dhamma, you cannot help somebody else to understand the dhamma.

63
00:06:39,882 --> 00:06:41,865
[SPEAKER_04]: That's one of the meanings of that phrase.

64
00:06:43,238 --> 00:06:45,980
[SPEAKER_04]: But you could also understand it in relation to what we just said.

65
00:06:46,521 --> 00:06:49,883
[SPEAKER_04]: If you yourself are not... and this is...

66
00:06:49,903 --> 00:06:52,446
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, that's the practical application of the sutta.

67
00:06:52,926 --> 00:07:01,032
[SPEAKER_04]: If you yourself are not tamed, trained and developed, you cannot tame, train and develop your mind.

68
00:07:02,214 --> 00:07:08,041
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means that all this hinges on making a distinction between you and mind.

69
00:07:08,261 --> 00:07:08,481
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

70
00:07:09,042 --> 00:07:17,912
[SPEAKER_03]: And it seems to me we should start there, clearing up precisely which mind you are not talking about, to begin with.

71
00:07:18,573 --> 00:07:28,316
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, we already had a discussion about it that, not to get too theoretical, but, you know, the suttas would make that distinction between mano and citta.

72
00:07:28,556 --> 00:07:28,757
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

73
00:07:29,757 --> 00:07:44,362
[SPEAKER_04]: And we actually had that talk in the past where we said that it defines the purity of mind as when there is sensuality, aversion,

74
00:07:45,523 --> 00:07:53,931
[SPEAKER_04]: The five hindrances internally, there is no... Basically, with Mano, you understand the escape from those things.

75
00:07:54,071 --> 00:07:54,292
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

76
00:07:54,392 --> 00:07:55,453
[SPEAKER_04]: Even though they have arisen.

77
00:07:55,853 --> 00:07:58,496
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you don't act out by body, speech and mind.

78
00:07:58,736 --> 00:07:59,056
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

79
00:07:59,237 --> 00:08:01,199
[SPEAKER_03]: Mano, and that is how...

80
00:08:01,819 --> 00:08:08,466
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, indirectly, the citta, which is, let's say, the background to that, can eventually calm down.

81
00:08:08,687 --> 00:08:13,852
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, so we defined that that's kind of the thing that you have direct control over.

82
00:08:14,613 --> 00:08:22,982
[SPEAKER_04]: So, initially, you know, breaking precepts, it doesn't matter what raging, feverish desires you have.

83
00:08:24,704 --> 00:08:27,527
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't do what your mind is pushing you to do.

84
00:08:27,647 --> 00:08:31,572
[SPEAKER_04]: And that already would start to highlight that gap.

85
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:36,637
[SPEAKER_04]: That, you know, even though you don't want these things to be there, they're still there.

86
00:08:37,298 --> 00:08:40,441
[SPEAKER_04]: And that kind of points to that aspect of, oh, citta is...

87
00:08:41,282 --> 00:08:43,464
[SPEAKER_04]: Something that's there on its own.

88
00:08:43,524 --> 00:08:44,945
[SPEAKER_04]: It's influenced by your choices.

89
00:08:45,565 --> 00:08:51,429
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, of course, you wouldn't be having those desires if you hadn't been acting in those ways in the past.

90
00:08:51,449 --> 00:08:51,669
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

91
00:08:51,709 --> 00:08:53,310
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not that it's randomly arisen.

92
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:54,571
[SPEAKER_04]: You're responsible for it.

93
00:08:55,372 --> 00:09:03,577
[SPEAKER_04]: But there is a kind of delay there, that you cannot, like, right away just tell it something and expect it to go along with it.

94
00:09:04,618 --> 00:09:04,878
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

95
00:09:06,139 --> 00:09:07,460
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this then shows two things.

96
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:10,342
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, one, that it is through virtue

97
00:09:11,603 --> 00:09:24,753
[SPEAKER_03]: Not as an idea, but actually conducting oneself, for example, in accordance with the eight precepts, that will be a foundation for experientially seeing this distinction between you and mind, citta.

98
00:09:25,194 --> 00:09:34,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Because mind wants to push you in one direction, but now you being committed to these precepts, the whole point of those precepts is precisely that they sort of come in between you and your desires, basically.

99
00:09:34,721 --> 00:09:35,342
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and...

100
00:09:35,422 --> 00:09:35,782
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

101
00:09:35,842 --> 00:09:40,206
[SPEAKER_04]: So, nothing that you might just think about abstractly,

102
00:09:41,006 --> 00:09:44,770
[SPEAKER_04]: No matter how much sense it makes, will actually point you to that distinction.

103
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:49,334
[SPEAKER_03]: No, you have to experience that for yourself through committing yourself to those precepts.

104
00:09:49,874 --> 00:09:52,697
[SPEAKER_03]: But, now you also mentioned that there is a bit of a delay.

105
00:09:53,157 --> 00:09:55,259
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there's also an element of faith here.

106
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:02,684
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you see, it might from the beginning not be quite clear why now add here to these eight precepts.

107
00:10:02,704 --> 00:10:05,505
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm just now just dogmatically abstracting all that.

108
00:10:05,866 --> 00:10:08,107
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, in the beginning that's not necessarily bad.

109
00:10:08,527 --> 00:10:14,930
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you do that with the view that that understanding will grow, you have to just take away those precepts.

110
00:10:15,431 --> 00:10:19,093
[SPEAKER_03]: And then another aspect of that faith is that you do not immediately

111
00:10:19,773 --> 00:10:21,935
[SPEAKER_03]: From the point of view of mind, see results.

112
00:10:21,975 --> 00:10:25,898
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it takes time for that animal to calm down.

113
00:10:26,659 --> 00:10:31,082
[SPEAKER_03]: But also, we have said earlier, that defilements are destroyed simply by not acting out.

114
00:10:31,743 --> 00:10:32,924
[SPEAKER_03]: This is also immediate.

115
00:10:33,204 --> 00:10:33,384
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

116
00:10:33,704 --> 00:10:34,985
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's layers.

117
00:10:35,326 --> 00:10:43,472
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, the bodily defilements, the verbal defilements, those are destroyed simply by you not making those choices.

118
00:10:44,193 --> 00:10:45,414
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why, as the Buddha said,

119
00:10:47,035 --> 00:11:00,229
[SPEAKER_04]: Even at the beginning of the training, if somebody has kind of an understanding of that virtue, they would be able to gain a degree of ease and peace from just that.

120
00:11:00,349 --> 00:11:07,097
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't mean that it's going to be enough for the right view, but they would be able to recognize, yeah, even though I may have these desires in my mind,

121
00:11:07,717 --> 00:11:13,641
[SPEAKER_04]: Just the fact that I no longer have to to act by body and speech.

122
00:11:13,701 --> 00:11:14,441
[SPEAKER_04]: Just imagine that.

123
00:11:14,481 --> 00:11:25,748
[SPEAKER_04]: Just imagine, like, you have a whole entire universe of things going on in your mind, but you never, ever act out of them by body and speech when they're unwholesome.

124
00:11:25,808 --> 00:11:28,970
[SPEAKER_04]: How many problems would be avoided just in account of that?

125
00:11:29,030 --> 00:11:29,370
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

126
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:33,913
[SPEAKER_04]: So... Yeah, so then...

127
00:11:36,036 --> 00:11:45,996
[SPEAKER_04]: The point there is, there's obviously that dimension of precepts and coarse behavior, but

128
00:11:47,356 --> 00:11:56,079
[SPEAKER_04]: When it comes to the mental dimension, that's when people would usually think that, somehow, that's different.

129
00:11:56,839 --> 00:12:01,120
[SPEAKER_04]: For that, you need concentration technique or something.

130
00:12:02,181 --> 00:12:04,621
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's actually the same exact principle.

131
00:12:05,142 --> 00:12:06,382
[SPEAKER_04]: Just that, as you said,

132
00:12:08,034 --> 00:12:13,397
[SPEAKER_04]: There is that delay, and you need to... there needs to be that element of faith to a degree.

133
00:12:13,477 --> 00:12:23,902
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you cannot be expecting immediate results, because... well, the only immediate result, really, that's that's going to come there is... is that you put the animal to sleep.

134
00:12:24,062 --> 00:12:24,943
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're not training it.

135
00:12:25,663 --> 00:12:28,664
[SPEAKER_04]: That's usually what, what... Yeah, with the concentration techniques.

136
00:12:28,744 --> 00:12:29,384
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

137
00:12:29,404 --> 00:12:31,125
[SPEAKER_04]: So, so, you, you just put it to sleep.

138
00:12:31,925 --> 00:12:33,365
[SPEAKER_04]: Of course, it stopped making noise.

139
00:12:34,025 --> 00:12:39,327
[SPEAKER_04]: It stops, eh, you know, destroying whatever is in its path.

140
00:12:39,467 --> 00:12:41,347
[SPEAKER_04]: But the point is that that doesn't train it.

141
00:12:41,947 --> 00:12:44,128
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's exactly why then, as soon as you stop,

142
00:12:44,970 --> 00:12:46,850
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't matter how calm it was.

143
00:12:46,890 --> 00:12:50,431
[SPEAKER_04]: It's going to go back to the way it was before, sooner or later.

144
00:12:51,792 --> 00:13:01,114
[SPEAKER_04]: So, instead, you need to give up that whole attitude of just immediate kind of appeasement of the animal.

145
00:13:01,774 --> 00:13:13,357
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, I mean, even like with like a child or something, if you... if your mechanism of getting your child to behave is to buy the toy that it wants,

146
00:13:14,357 --> 00:13:23,844
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, that works for now, but it's not a long-term good parenting strategy.

147
00:13:24,125 --> 00:13:24,525
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

148
00:13:25,025 --> 00:13:26,847
[SPEAKER_04]: So, same with your mind.

149
00:13:28,007 --> 00:13:31,430
[SPEAKER_04]: You can have sensual desire, aversion.

150
00:13:31,750 --> 00:13:36,894
[SPEAKER_04]: So, of course, the fact that you don't... that's where usually people don't

151
00:13:38,515 --> 00:13:43,779
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't have the nuanced thinking to recognize that it's more subtle than just...

152
00:13:43,799 --> 00:13:47,061
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so I don't get rid of it means like I just allow it to be there.

153
00:13:47,481 --> 00:13:51,004
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so there is something in between the punishment and reward mentality.

154
00:13:51,084 --> 00:13:52,044
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, yeah.

155
00:13:52,725 --> 00:13:57,188
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't reward it if you want it to be quiet, nor do you punish it if you want it to be quiet.

156
00:13:57,228 --> 00:14:00,470
[SPEAKER_03]: But, well, you have to endure whatever mood the child might be in.

157
00:14:00,730 --> 00:14:00,931
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

158
00:14:01,131 --> 00:14:03,933
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you don't have punishment and reward, which

159
00:14:04,773 --> 00:14:05,854
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, what are you going to do?

160
00:14:05,894 --> 00:14:13,997
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, recognizing that it is a human being, capable of understanding things, you could, for example, point out the consequences of behavior that is undesirable.

161
00:14:14,717 --> 00:14:19,479
[SPEAKER_03]: Then the child can come to the conclusion for itself that it is better to not do that.

162
00:14:20,239 --> 00:14:27,022
[SPEAKER_03]: And then also, with that, it becomes more independent, rather than just mirroring something, hoping for a reward.

163
00:14:27,322 --> 00:14:27,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

164
00:14:28,526 --> 00:14:42,296
[SPEAKER_04]: And in that teaching relationship, you would need to not be shouting and angry and clustered, because that doesn't help.

165
00:14:42,536 --> 00:14:44,918
[SPEAKER_04]: It's going to start doing stuff out of fear.

166
00:14:45,758 --> 00:14:54,562
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because, you know, it's actually understanding what you're saying, but just because, okay, I wanted to calm down, so I'm just gonna do what he's saying.

167
00:14:55,042 --> 00:15:04,086
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not really how you get someone to see the harm of what they're doing.

168
00:15:04,446 --> 00:15:10,469
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in this case, going back to the example, yeah, you have a thought of ill will.

169
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:15,563
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the natural strategy to get rid of that?

170
00:15:15,663 --> 00:15:17,946
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I just start forcefully.

171
00:15:18,126 --> 00:15:21,749
[SPEAKER_04]: For you, it might not feel forceful, but from the ultimate point of view, it is.

172
00:15:22,350 --> 00:15:27,035
[SPEAKER_04]: You start thinking thoughts of metta, thoughts of goodwill, thoughts of loving-kindness.

173
00:15:29,372 --> 00:15:31,033
[SPEAKER_04]: Which, in the surface, are kind of good.

174
00:15:31,253 --> 00:15:39,039
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, these are the things that you would want the mind to incline to, but you don't realize that you're kind of force-feeding those things to the mind.

175
00:15:39,099 --> 00:15:44,302
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you yourself are not like... you're not stable.

176
00:15:44,482 --> 00:15:47,744
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not equanimous towards what's going on.

